SM(519,{"n":"Romans 11","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 11.mp3","d":2398.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.84,"Okay, so I will get started here in just a minute."],[25.52,"When we get started, we're gonna be picking up at the beginning of St."],[28.96,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, chapter 11."],[31.84,"So, but since it we have had a little bit of a break first, I will get us sort of caught up by uh running real quickly through sort of this section of Romans really begins in chapter eight, and so we'll sort of go from real quickly through eight, nine, and ten before we start into eleven."],[51.68,"So we're not sort of starting in the middle."],[53.84,"So in chapter eight, Saint Paul talked about the theme of us becoming sons of God."],[62.04,"And we talked about how sons of God was actually a reference to a particular rank or type of angels in the Old Testament."],[72.28,"And so that this is talking about, this isn't just talking about sort of a nice God's our dad, right kind of thing, but that God's family is a royal family."],[83.1,"And so becoming sons of God means we become heirs."],[86.78,"It's the language uses heirs along with Christ to the kingdom."],[91.02,"Because as you imagine, the the children of the king."],[94.59,"You have one heir on earth."],[97.55,"And Saint Paul has set up Christ as Christ is the heir."],[101.08,"He's the firstborn of all things."],[103.0,"But we, by becoming sons of God, become heirs along with Christ of the kingdom."],[109.52,"And not only does that mean that we inherit the kingdom, but that God shares with us in ruling over it."],[117.6,"And we talked about some of the other passages in the Gospels that also talk about that."],[124.72,"And that's the reason he uses the language sons, instead of children for a couple of reasons."],[130.74,"It's not just that he's sexist."],[132.68,"Because you can see in other places, he uses the language children of God."],[134.84,"uses the language children of God. Uh, and Saint John of God more often than sons"],[142.4,"But he uses sons for two reasons."],[144.96,"One reason being again to refer to that class of angels, and angels aren't men and women."],[151.54,"So sons of God was just a title in the Old Testament."],[154.82,"And also because in the culture in which St."],[157.3,"Paul is writing, it was the son who was the heir who inherited."],[162.5,"So it's not that St."],[164.5,"Paul is only talking to men."],[167.12,"It's that Christians, both men and women, become sons in the sense that they become heirs."],[174.49,"So there's not sort of this division, even though on earth, especially in St."],[178.73,"Paul's time, there's this division where it's the son who inherits everything, and the daughters."],[183.65,"Well, hopefully they go get married to somebody the son."],[188.13,"Get that in."],[189.29,"But in in the kingdom of God, that. we all become co-heirs with Christ"],[195.81,"So then in chapter 9, he gave the example remember of Jacob and Esau, where he gave he put right next to each other two quotes about Esau."],[209.01,"The first one was from before Jacob and Esau, remember they were twins."],[214.37,"And uh Esau came out first, which meant he was the firstborn, which meant he was the heir."],[219.97,"Jacob came out second."],[221.41,"But the prophecy at the time they were born was that the older would serve the younger."],[226.01,"And then he gives the quote from much later in the prophets, from centuries and centuries later, Jacob I have loved, Esau I have hated."],[234.85,"Which in context, we saw was talking. about the nation of Edom that was descended from Esau, as opposed to the nation of Israel"],[242.49,"Because remember, Israel was Jacob's other name."],[245.53,"And so we talked about how St."],[248.49,"Paul is appealing to sort of the whole history of those nations."],[251.93,"And what you see is, for example, when you go to Deuteronomy 2, and God has brought the people of Israel out of Egypt, and he's bringing them to the land, they come through Edom, and he says, You're not to attack the people of Edom."],[266.01,"You're not to take even a foot of their land, because they're your brothers, and God has given them this land."],[271.15,"Which was part of the land that he promised to Abraham."],[274.35,"Because Esau was Abraham's descendant, too."],[277.15,"And so it talks about how they dealt peacefully with each other. and how they were brothers"],[282.23,"And then later on in the history of Israel, Edom became basically a vassal state of Israel."],[288.55,"That prophecy, the older will serve the younger."],[291.67,"But as long as that faithfulness, that faith and loyalty and fellowship was maintained between the descendants of Esau and the descendants of Israel, the descendants of Esau received part of the inheritance."],[305.79,"Along with along with Israel."],[308.03,"Then a time came later, reflected in that second prophecy that St."],[311.95,"Paul quoted, where when the time came that the Assyrians destroyed the northern kingdom of Israel, and then the Babylonians came and destroyed the kingdom of Judah, rather than helping their brothers, the people of Israel, the Edomites chose to attack them, use that as an opportunity to take more land."],[333.05,"They turned on them."],[334.65,"And so because they had now turned on the people of Israel, they lost their inheritance too."],[341.13,"And that's what God prophesied against them was that now, because of the way they had treated Israel, they were going to lose the land that he had given them as well."],[349.61,"In terms of today's geography, would Edom be in southern Jordan?"],[354.41,"Yes. Yeah, Petra was an Edomite capital."],[357.85,"Okay So the analogy that St."],[361.37,"Paul is making here, is remember the question Saint Paul was addressing when he brought up that example is well, how come if J ifesus Jesus is the Messiah, if Jesus is the King of the Jews, is the one who's promised all through the Hebrew scriptures, why did so many of St."],[377.97,"Paul's fellow Jews reject him?"],[379.97,"How does that work?"],[381.09,"What does that mean?"],[382.53,"And so he's using this example to say, look, they're, yeah, they're descendants of Abraham."],[390.14,"But because they've cut themselves off from the heir, who is Jesus Christ, because they've cut themselves off from him, they lose that inheritance."],[399.18,"Just like the Edomites."],[400.62,"Just like the Edomites lost, lost their part of the inheritance by cutting themselves off from Israel."],[407.1,"So, in the same way that receiving part of that inheritance from Abraham depended on keeping faith with God's firstborn Israel, now receiving that inheritance depends on keeping faith with Christ, who is God's firstborn."],[422.44,"And is the heir?"],[423.56,"So again, he's going back to what he said in chapter 8."],[426.04,"We become co-heirs with Christ by keeping faith with Christ."],[430.68,"We don't receive anything for ourselves because we're descended from Abraham or not."],[437.24,"And so then he moved on at the end of chapter 9 to address the question that he's going to come back to here in chapter 11, which is why did God do what he did?"],[451.72,"This is still an open question."],[453.0,"He's still talking about it when we come to chapter 11."],[455.56,"Why did God do what he did in terms of Israel then?"],[458.84,"So, for example, God brings all twelve tribes out of Egypt."],[462.64,"He brings them to the land."],[464.8,"A couple hundred years later, the northern kingdom of Israel no longer exists."],[469.6,"That's ten tribes."],[472.72,"Gone. So why did God bring them out of Egypt at the first place?"],[477.58,"Because he knew."],[479.42,"He knew that was gonna happen."],[481.02,"So why'd he bother bringing them out of Israel, bringing them out of Egypt?"],[486.22,"Why did he bother giving them a government?"],[489.66,"You know, when the northern kingdom separated, he went to Jeroboam, their first king, Jeroboam, son of Nebat, he said, Oh, if you keep all my laws and keep all my ways, I'll establish your dynasty forever, like I did with David."],[501.02,"Well, God knew when he said that that Jeroboam wasn't going to do it."],[503.18,"He knew Jeroboam was going to be wicked"],[508.18,"So, see, Paul's saying, Well, why?"],[510.34,"Why all this?"],[511.48,"Why all this history?"],[513.08,"And he knew what was going to happen to Judah and Benjamin."],[515.4,"He knew, he knew all this."],[517.48,"Why bother? And he's and in that context, so far, what he's talked about is he's talked about the fact that God has preserved sort of this remnant."],[531.9,"There is this part of the descendants of Abraham of Israel that has stayed, that has accepted embraced Christ."],[538.83,"Embrace Christ when he came."],[541.27,"So it's not everyone."],[543.0,"So there is this core, this remnant."],[545.34,"But that doesn't really answer the question of okay, there's this remnant, but then why the other ninety-five percent or whatever the percentage would be, 90%, 80%, whatever it is, why the rest of that?"],[557.94,"Why bother giving them all these blessings in the first place when you knew they were going to?"],[563.75,"And so the only way he's addressed that so far is to kind of point out that the question you're asking is why did God give blessings to these people he knew would be ungrateful?"],[575.19,"Why did he ever do anything good for them?"],[578.73,"Which is kind of a weird question when you think about it."],[582.5,"Why would God be good to them ever?"],[584.58,"Why didn't he just, you know, never create them or you know, wipe wipe them out ear?lier"],[589.98,"Well, you know."],[593.42,"But that's not to say that St."],[595.18,"Paul doesn't think there isn't a purpose."],[597.18,"And we're going to get to that in chapter 11, is where he's going to sort of finish his answer to that question."],[603.82,"And so then in chapter 10, he talks about the fact that through all of these people being cut off, even though all these people from Israel have been cut off, there have been all these Gentiles, people from the other nations, who have now come to faith in Christ."],[624.66,"And who now have this relationship of faithfulness with Christ."],[629.06,"And we talked about how important it is that we translate."],[632.2,"We're used to hearing faingith faith, and we're used to think of as just like, oh yeah, I believe that Jesus is the Messiah."],[633.16,"True That's not what faith is biblically."],[640.79,"It's faithfulness, it's loyalty."],[642.65,"It's ongoing, it's the way we we live our life."],[645.69,"And so the people who have been cut off are cut off because they've broken faith with Christ."],[651.77,"They've cut themselves off."],[653.37,"And so these other people who have now become faithful and loyal to Christ are now a part."],[660.27,"And so are becoming, are becoming co-heirs."],[663.63,"And so right at the end of chapter 10, he gave uh St"],[670.03,"Paul quoted a whole series of prophecies that prophesied that this would happen, this being that the Gentiles would come. in"],[678.23,"Right, where he says, and we can look at a few of these at the end of chapter 10, uh, where he quotes from Deuteronomy, I will provoke you to jealousy by those who are not a nation, I will move you to anger by a foolish nation."],[692.79,"And in Isaiah, I was found by those who did not seek me, I was made manifest to those who did not ask for me."],[699.67,"And then finally, he quotes Joel, which says, All day long, I have stretched out my hands to a disobedient and contrary people."],[707.99,"Talking about Israel."],[710.23,"So he he is also making the point that God didn't turn his back on Israel."],[718.23,"God didn't turn his back on them."],[719.79,"They turned their back on him."],[721.11,"He's still there"],[723.79,"If they're willing to come to Christ, then they'll be they'll be a part of what he's doing as well."],[731.31,"And so one last note before we start chapter 11 here, because I made this point before, but it's been a while."],[737.15,"St. Paul, there are many things where we've gone through and we've said he uses like body and flesh."],[745.31,"We think of those as the same thing, but he uses them differently."],[748.35,"Well, another one of those is Israel and the Jews."],[752.59,"Especially in our modern world, we think those are the same thing."],[756.27,"Especially in our modern political discourse."],[758.51,"If you say anything negative about the country of Israel, you're anti-Semitic and attacking the Jewish people."],[763.37,"These are two different terms."],[765.05,"o Okaykay? Especially then, because at the time St."],[768.21,"Paul's writing, there was no Israel."],[771.17,"There was no nation called Israel."],[774.37,"There hadn't been for 750 years."],[778.61,"It had been 750 years since there was a nation called Israel in the world."],[782.29,"So that's not what he's talking about."],[784.69,"He's not talking about a country."],[786.29,"There's Israel and then the Jews."],[788.05,"He uses both terms."],[791.81,"Literally, the word that we translate as the Jews means the Judeans, the people who live in Judea, the Roman province."],[799.17,"And usually when St."],[800.45,"Paul uses it, he's also including Galileans."],[805.23,"This is one of those trivia trick questions."],[807.87,"Jesus technically was not a Jew."],[809.86,"Different province in Rome"],[810.03,"Because he was not a Judean, he was a Galilean."],[816.91,"But um, but so he usually, when St."],[820.75,"Paul's using that, he's referring to Judean and Galilean people and people of that ethnicity who live other places."],[828.19,"That's what he means when he says Jews."],[830.43,"When he's talking about Israel, he's speaking theologically."],[836.21,"He's talking about all twelve tribes."],[839.49,"He's talking about Israel the way it's talked about in the Old Testament."],[843.11,"The Israel of God, he's gonna say."],[845.27,"Paul is writing"],[845.55,"And that's important because again, this is not a thing that exists when St."],[851.63,"When he's talking about all twelve tribes, ten of those tribes do not exist."],[857.71,"So does he speak in I think he's not speaking historically only."],[862.99,"No. He's not talking about the past."],[865.31,"He's talking about it."],[866.43,"But but the reason I'm hammering on this is as we've been reading of him talking about Israel, as we're gonna hear him talk about Israel in chapter 11."],[873.79,"This has nothing to do with any nation that has ever or will ever exist."],[879.87,"This has nothing to do with ethnicity."],[882.75,"He's specifically addressing there's a whole group of prophecies, and we won't go through them all now."],[888.11,"In Isaiah, Amos, and some of the other prophets, that when they talk about God coming to his people and restoring them, it talks about all twelve tribes."],[897.71,"It talks about tribes that at the time the prophecy was written didn't exist anymore."],[901.83,"Talks about Ephraim being restored, which was the central tribe, the biggest tribe of the northern tribes."],[907.75,"That's where the capital was."],[909.03,"They promised these things."],[910.71,"So part of what St."],[911.75,"Paul is doing here when he's talking about Israel is how are those prophecies going to be fulfilled?"],[917.11,"How can that possibly happen since those tribes no longer exist?"],[921.19,"There is no Manasseh, there is no Ephraim, there is no Dan, there is no."],[925.11,"And he's gonna he's gonna give an answer here in chapter 11 as to how that happens."],[930.79,"But those are not prophecies that anyone, any of the New Testament writers, when you get to the book of Revelation, St."],[938.07,"John is gonna talk about, he's gonna list all the tribes."],[940.55,"And you're like, well, those tribes don't exist."],[942.73,"So all the New Testament writers see those prophecies as being fulfilled."],[947.57,"None of them see them being fulfilled in terms of some kind of Jewish nation state."],[955.15,"Not one of them."],[957.95,"So are they, I'm this is jumping ahead a bit, but are they fulfilled in the replacement of these people with the well we'll we'll get there."],[970.83,"We'll get there."],[973.07,"So unless there are any questions or comments or allegations or outbursts, uh, we'll go ahead and get started in uh Romans chapter 11, verse 1."],[985.63,"I say then, has God cast away his people?"],[989.27,"Certainly not. For I also am an Israelite of the seed of Abraham of the tribe of Benjamin."],[996.07,"Okay. So that that certainly not is this sort of this Greek phrase, uh me guinnito, which St."],[1003.91,"Paul, it's it's a weird phrase because it's an outdated form of the language."],[1008.31,"It's what's called an optative verb."],[1010.55,"And there's like 60 times that they're used in the whole New Testament, and 40 some of them are Saint Paul saying meek any tongue."],[1020.39,"It's just sort of this expression he uses."],[1023.15,"Which literally means something like, may it never be, may it not be."],[1027.67,"God forbid. God forbid in the in the King James, they translated it that way."],[1032.31,"way. No Certainly not here."],[1035.07,"You know. I'm sure in some modern translation somewhere."],[1040.27,"But the idea that this is something we don't want to ever happen."],[1043.31,"And so he's making the same point he just made at the end of chapter 10."],[1047.64,"Did God cast away his people?"],[1050.76,"Because that would that would be one way to interpret what had happened."],[1054.2,"One way to interpret what had happened would be well, God's just done with Israel."],[1059.88,"That's over. They're gone."],[1062.28,"Now it's the Gentiles."],[1064.86,"That whole Old Testament thing is over."],[1067.58,"He's starting over."],[1068.96,"St. Paul's saying that's not that's not what's happening."],[1072.8,"And he's saying that he himself is proof of that, because he's a descendant of Abraham."],[1078.86,"He's from the tribe of Benjamin, he says."],[1081.02,"And he obviously has accepted Christ."],[1085.28,"And is a faithful follower of Christ."],[1087.52,"So that means God hasn't jettisoned the whole people."],[1093.12,"He says, God has not cast away his people whom he foreknew."],[1097.2,"Or do you not know what the scripture says of Elijah, how he pleads with God against Israel, saying, Lord, they have killed your prophets and torn down your altars, and I alone am left, and they seek my life."],[1111.6,"But what does the divine response say to him?"],[1113.92,"I have reserved for myself seven thousand men who have not bowed the knee to Baal."],[1119.6,"Okay, so St."],[1120.16,"Elias Paul goes back to this story about Elijah about St."],[1125.53,"This is right after the big showdown at Mount Carmel, where he and the prophet of prophets of Baal had this big showdown where they both built altars and God sent fire from heaven."],[1136.33,"Which I hate to ruin your Sunday school, but fire from heaven is how they referred to lightning."],[1145.29,"Aww. Yeah. So that's what happened."],[1151.29,"And the reason that was important was if you remember leading up to that, it hadn't rained for several years."],[1156.49,"Uh-huh That's because that's because Baal was a storm god."],[1160.73,"So they had all stopped worshiping their God and gone to worshiping Baal, this storm god."],[1164.81,"'s. And so the real god said, okay, well, it not going to rain anymore"],[1171.49,"So who's in control of that?"],[1173.17,"And so if you're a storm god, what do they always depict Baal, Zeus, everybody's holding a lightning bolt?"],[1179.49,"And so the idea of the showdown on Mount Carmel was we'll both build altars, we'll both put animals on them, and we'll see which god sends fire from heaven, sends sends lightning to light it, to set it on fire."],[1192.53,"And so yeah, that's what's lightning."],[1195.01,"It was lightning."],[1197.81,"But uh so right after that happened, and then after he won, Elijah slew a bunch of the prophets of Baal."],[1206.05,"After that, he goes off by himself, and he sort of has this depressed come down moment."],[1213.95,"Where he prays and he says, you know, look, you know, yeah, we just won that one on Mount Carmel, but the truth is they've gone and killed all the other prophets except me, they've torn down all the altars, you know, they're all worshiping Baal."],[1229.15,"You know, I'm the last person left."],[1231.47,"Who cares? You know, and basically he prays and says, God, why don't you just go ahead and kill me?"],[1237.31,"Because yeah, because I can't I can't do this anymore."],[1241.47,"And God's response, he sort of rebukes him."],[1243.89,"And says, I've reserved for myself 7,000 men who have not bowed the knee to Baal."],[1247.87,"So meaning you're not the only one."],[1249.69,"There are thousands of people who are still faithful."],[1253.02,"And what St."],[1253.86,"Paul is saying is that that's probably the first place in the Hebrew scriptures where we see this idea of there being a remnant."],[1263.94,"So, first of all, it's not a new thing that the majority of the Israelites would be apostates."],[1270.56,"Look, in Elijah's day, the majority of Israel were apostates."],[1276.96,"That's been true all along that the majority has been disobedient."],[1280.48,"But God has also all along, just like then, preserved this remnant."],[1287.12,"And so St."],[1287.76,"Paul's saying the same is true now."],[1289.6,"I know you look out there, and it looks like the Jewish people have all turned their back on Christ, their Messiah."],[1299.3,"But the truth is it's not all of them."],[1303.06,"It's not all of them."],[1304.42,"In fact, it's about the same proportion as you see all through Israel's history."],[1308.58,"That the faithful few are a faithful few."],[1312.1,"Even so, then, at this present time, there is a remnant according to the election of grace."],[1316.98,"And if by grace that it is no longer of works, otherwise grace is no longer grace."],[1322.66,"But if it is of works, it is no longer grace, otherwise work is no longer work."],[1328.5,"So there's a nice Yeah, you got a little work to do. there"],[1331.34,"Th'eres a nice uh a nice tug twister for you."],[1334.02,"Okay But what he's saying is, remember, we've talked already in Romans about what the de definition of grace is."],[1340.42,"Grace is God working."],[1341.86,"ing. So this is what he's address"],[1342.46,"God's action. He says, Is this by God's action or by human action?"],[1350.16,"And he's been saying all along, this is by God's action."],[1355.68,"Remember, we talked about how uh the word that we translate predestined, which has caused so many problems, actually literally means put in order before time."],[1364.96,"Sometimes that's translated for ordained, to give that idea."],[1368.48,"But the idea is Christ died for me before I existed."],[1374.4,"Before I existed, before I did anything, God prepared salvation for us before we existed."],[1383.12,"Because he knew we would, because he created us."],[1386.42,"So he knew, and because he knew that his people would exist, he set things in order."],[1393.34,"He did these things, he accomplished these things in Christ beforehand."],[1398.7,"Beforehand, so that we would have the way of salvation available to us."],[1405.15,"And so the same thing here."],[1407.63,"This isn't that, oh, there's this remnant, and they're just better than the other ones."],[1413.76,"It's that there's this remnant, and the reason this remnant is preserved, as he just quoted, I've preserved for myself."],[1421.3,"Is this the grace of God?"],[1423.14,"He had prepared the means."],[1424.58,"He obviously is the Messiah and came as the Messiah."],[1430.87,"Came to them."],[1431.99,"That was grace."],[1433.03,"They didn't earn it."],[1434.15,"They didn't do anything to make him come."],[1437.24,"Um, and that's running counter to remember what the Pharisees believed."],[1442.04,"Pharisees believed when Israel becomes righteous again."],[1445.16,"Remember, their math was the reason we got sent into exile is that we were sinful and did keep the law."],[1451.24,"So once we're all keeping the law perfectly and we're all righteous, then the Messiah will come and God will save us."],[1457.89,"That's what they believed."],[1459.25,"They believed it worked that way."],[1461.09,"And St. Paul's been saying over and over again, no, it's the exact opposite way."],[1466.05,"God doesn't come to Israel and Egypt and say, okay, here's the law."],[1469.81,"When you keep it, I'll deliver you from slavery."],[1473.3,"He takes them out of slavery, he brings them to the land and just gives it to them."],[1477.22,"And then he says, Okay, now here's the law, this is what I expect from you."],[1482.6,"Which is faithfulness."],[1484.92,"I have a question that's sort of well, I guess it has to do with Protestantism."],[1490.84,"The um formerly Baptist now independent church in Broadbridge has this sign thing out front, of course."],[1501.0,"And lately it's been saying, very few go to heaven."],[1505.96,"Uh are you going to be one of them, or something like that."],[1509.48,"But the the beginning is the very few going to heaven."],[1515.04,"Um that's that sounds like this remnant talk, but I don't think it really is."],[1523.36,"So can you explain right."],[1525.46,"Well, we don't know how many people are or aren't going to go to heaven."],[1534.9,"We don't have the exact numbers."],[1537.79,"So they may be trying to get across the idea, like in Christ's parable where he says, broad is the way and easy is the path that leads to destruction, but narrow is the way, and difficult is the path that leads to life, and few find it."],[1552.27,"They may be trying to get across that idea."],[1555.64,"The idea that, which is the the core idea of that parable, is the idea that we don't get to the kingdom of God by living an easy, lazy life."],[1568.14,"Just sort of doing what we want, that it takes work, it takes effort, on our part to seek it out and find it."],[1574.98,"So it's a more difficult road."],[1576.9,"That may be the idea they're getting from, you know, that they're trying to convey."],[1583.06,"You know, so you should come to church because otherwise you're gonna wander off to destruction, you know, kind of idea."],[1590.1,"Um, but yeah, that's not the same idea."],[1593.94,"Well, this the remnant is a very particular thing, sort of in Old Testament prophecy in terms of Israel."],[1601.62,"I don't know that I would try to apply that now."],[1607.02,"I wouldn't. They might."],[1610.3,"They might have the idea that, you know, I mean, there are there are some Protestant groups that sort of have the idea that they're the only ones who have got it right, and so they're sort of the faithful remnant."],[1623.66,"You know. Um, in fact, I think I've even seen a Faithful Remnant Baptist Church once."],[1629.58,"But but that's not what St."],[1633.98,"Paul is dealing with it."],[1635.34,"That this remnant is a remnant of Old Testament Israel, uh-huh, which is purified and becomes then the basis and the root for the church."],[1647.34,"So it's a particular moment here"],[1652.82,"So the Gentiles that come in and join."],[1657.7,"Well, we'll get there."],[1658.98,"We're getting there."],[1660.02,"Okay, but not there yet."],[1661.14,"Yeah, we're getting there."],[1662.74,"But so this whole thing about grace and works, the reason he brings it up again is to prevent a possible misinterpretation of what he's saying."],[1670.2,"Because one of his fellow Pharisees could come along and say, Oh, okay, I see what you're saying."],[1675.56,"There were a group of us who were righteous and who were keeping the law perfectly."],[1680.52,"And so God sent the Messiah for us, and he just doesn't care about the rest of these people."],[1685.36,"Like that's a possible interpretation of what St."],[1688.96,"Paul is saying."],[1690.46,"That God only cares about the elect."],[1690.62,"God only cares about this small group."],[1695.54,"Notice that's a misinterpretation of what St."],[1697.62,"Paul is saying."],[1702.42,"And so he's making the point no no no no no no no no no."],[1706.82,"That's not the basis here for who accepted Christ and who didn't."],[1712.18,"In fact, he's already made the argument that the whole approach of the Pharisees to righteousness, to their own self righteousness, is a big part of what ended up causing them to reject Christ because of their own self-righteous attitude."],[1734.82,"So in verse 7, what then?"],[1737.58,"Israel has not attained what it seeks, but the elect have obtained it, and the rest were blinded."],[1745.1,"Just as it is written, God has given them a spirit of stupor, eyes that they should not see, and ears that they should not hear to this very day."],[1753.82,"And David says, Let their table become a snare and a trap, a stumbling block and a recompense to them."],[1759.5,"Let their eyes be darkened so that they do not see, and bow down their back always."],[1766.38,"Here we get to an idea that we talked about back in St."],[1769.42,"John's Gospel. Some folks have interpreted this to mean, oh, well, God chose to give salvation to these people, and then God deliberately chose to make these other people blind so they wouldn't accept Christ."],[1780.3,"to make So they want interpret this as the people didn't any choices."],[1790.42,"God just chose to chuck the lion's share of Israel, and he chose just these few who he wanted."],[1800.82,"Now, first of all, that contradicts what he was just saying."],[1804.2,"That God has not turned his back on his people, but all day long he stretched out his hands to them."],[1809.5,"So it contradicts what he just said."],[1811.5,"But in terms of understanding what he's actually saying, we can go back to Romans 1, where in Romans 1 he was talking about the Gentiles."],[1819.26,"And you remember what he said there?"],[1820.94,"He talked about how the Gentiles had the nations had turned to idolatry. and worshipping other gods"],[1828.58,"And because they had rejected God and worshiped these other gods, and because they were wicked and sinful, St."],[1835.7,"Paul says God gave them over."],[1838.5,"And so then there's this long list of sins, sexual immorality, theft."],[1843.08,"Everything else, you know, every other kind of kind of sin that he lists that God just gave them over to."],[1849.24,"But he didn't just decide, you know, I don't care about the Greeks, I don't care about the Romans, I don't care about the Chinese, I'm going to give them over to sin and wickedness and send them all to hell."],[1860.37,"That's not what St."],[1861.17,"Paul is saying."],[1861.97,"St. Paul is saying because they rejected God, because they turned away from God, because they said they did not want him."],[1868.93,"He said, okay, you don't want me"],[1869.61,"He basically gave them what they wanted."],[1875.65,"Okay. Here's what you want."],[1878.13,"And what they wanted turned out to be destruction."],[1882.13,"It ended up to be havoc, sin, destruction."],[1886.23,"That destroyed, that destroyed their lives."],[1888.79,"And even the Gentiles to whom he's writing knew that the things he was listing were morally wrong."],[1895.83,"They had a concept of morality."],[1897.51,"So they had to look around at their fellow people and say, W,ell yeah, all those things are sort of running rampant."],[1905.99,"St. Paul's saying too."],[1907.51,"That's a result of your rejection of the only true God."],[1912.31,"And so this is what this is what St."],[1916.67,"Paul is saying here."],[1917.47,"It's the same thing St."],[1918.43,"John made clear in his gospel."],[1920.03,"We saw that over and over again, that the way people reacted when they encountered Jesus was based on who they already were, who they had already become."],[1929.87,"The ones who were living lives seeking to follow God, and who were faithful to him, when they encountered Jesus, they recognized him instantly, who he was, and they became his followers and his disciples."],[1942.93,"The ones who were wicked in their hearts and their souls, when they encountered Christ, they rejected him."],[1948.93,"But the reason they accepted or rejected him was based on the person they already were."],[1953.49,"And that's what St."],[1954.53,"Paul is saying here."],[1956.34,"That because these people had already rejected God, God gave them over to what they wanted."],[1963.18,"And so even when the Messiah was standing right in front of him, they couldn't see him or understand him or know who he was."],[1971.44,"Because they'd already rejected God and his ways."],[1975.2,"And those things."],[1976.08,"So their blindness is the result of their own choices that they've already made, their own faithlessness."],[1984.16,"And that's what happened here."],[1985.92,"It's in the same dynamic you see in Exodus with Pharaoh as the plagues start coming."],[1992.58,"At first he's hardening his own heart."],[1994.98,"And then it says God hardens his heart."],[1997.54,"Because there comes a certain point where God, okay."],[2001.14,"If this is what you want, you're gonna get all of it"],[2006.7,"You're gonna get all of it."],[2009.34,"So yeah, this is this is from St."],[2012.7,"Paul's perspective, this is the great threat."],[2015.66,"The great the thing we should pray that never happens is that God give us what God gives us what we think we want."],[2022.3,"What we think we want will probably destroy us."],[2026.14,"Rather than trusting him to give us what we need."],[2029.18,"So I should stop buying lottery tickets."],[2033.74,"Yeah, it's gambling."],[2034.78,"It's bad. Just enter the publisher's clearinghouse sweepstakes."],[2038.22,"You don't have to buy anything."],[2039.74,"And you have about the same chance of winning something."],[2042.82,"So But yeah, see, we tend to say, here W'ells what I want, and if God doesn't give it to me, then we get mad and we complain."],[2050.28,"We say, Why doesn't God listen to my prayers?"],[2054.12,"Actually, we usually say I really need this when we don't."],[2057.53,"Rather than trusting God to give us what we need and thanking him for it."],[2061.86,"And sometimes thanking him."],[2063.3,"Sometimes we look back at our life, at least I'll speak for myself."],[2065.86,"I look back at my life and some of the things I prayed and asked him for."],[2069.14,"It would have been a disaster if he'd given it to me."],[2072.02,"You know? So I'm actually thankful he didn't answer some of those prayers."],[2076.7,"It sort of didn't give me some of those things I thought I wanted at the time."],[2081.0,"Because he spared me a lot."],[2082.96,"But yeah, so this is on the negative side, this is what has happened to those people who have now separated from Christ. is that God has given them what they thought they wanted"],[2094.3,"And it's a it's a terrible thing for them."],[2097.1,"So verse 11, I say then, have they stumbled that they should fall?"],[2102.06,"Certainly not, again."],[2104.7,"But through their fall to provoke them to jealousy, salvation has come to the Gentiles."],[2120.62,"And St. Paul's saying, Well, of course not."],[2122.62,"Of course not."],[2124.62,"In fact, hopefully, now that they see the Gentiles coming to faith and following Christ, they'll be moved to jealousy, and maybe that will draw them back."],[2135.8,"When they see what God is doing among the Gentiles, that He's He's fulfilling these promises among them."],[2143.32,"Now, if their fall is riches for the world and their failure riches for riches for the Gentiles, how much more their fullness?"],[2152.6,"So he's saying, you know, if them having fallen away, you know, now the Gentiles have come in, and we're we rejoice about how the Gentiles have come in, how much more wonderful will it be if they now also come to faith in Christ?"],[2168.5,"So this is another possible, again, Saint Paul is here still attacking this idea that he started out chapter 11 attacking, which is the idea that God just said, okay, this whole Israel thing is over with."],[2178.02,"The whole Old Testament thing is over with, I'm starting over"],[2185.42,"Is he saying basically we don't know how he might bring these people back?"],[2192.78,"Here's some possibilities, but he was at first."],[2197.9,"St. Paul himself was persecuting the church."],[2200.38,"He was cut off and ended up coming to faithfulness in Christ."],[2205.5,"So he's going to say elsewhere, if he can, anybody can come to follow Christ."],[2212.38,"But so the argument here in chapter 11 so far is he says, well, okay, is he just done with Israel then?"],[2217.34,"Well, no, first of all, there's this remnant."],[2219.67,"He says, Th'eres me, but it's not just me."],[2222.23,"There are a lot of people who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[2227.13,"And now he's saying, and God's not even done with those who haven't accepted him yet."],[2232.81,"The fact that there are still to this day Jewish people who have not accepted Jesus as the Messiah doesn't mean that any one of them or any number of them couldn't come and accept, and there have been all through history Jewish people who have become Christian."],[2247.37,"He says, For I speak to you Gentiles, and as much as I am an apostle to the Gentiles, I magnify my ministry, if by any means I may provoke to jealousy those who are my flesh and save some of them."],[2260.17,"So he says, right now I'm writing to you."],[2263.63,"A lot of the church in Rome."],[2265.71,"Remember, what occasioned this letter is that Claudius had cast all the Jews out of Rome, out of the city of Rome."],[2275.11,"Okay. So the Christians who are Gentiles had stayed in the city, but the Jews were gone for over a year."],[2284.15,"And now they've come back."],[2285.91,"Now that they're coming back, St."],[2287.35,"Paul wants to make sure that we don't end up with a Jewish church and a Gentile church, but that we have all one church there now, for a year over a year, has been just Gentiles."],[2303.65,"So he says, I'm talking to you, who are in authority in the church now, who are Gentiles."],[2308.35,"In as much as he says he's the apostle to the Gentiles."],[2310.59,"Remember, apostle means one sent."],[2312.63,"And the way St."],[2313.51,"Paul uses it is very close to the idea of a prophet."],[2316.88,"A prophet is sent."],[2318.38,"And most of the prophets in the Old Testament were sent to the people of Israel."],[2322.84,"He's saying, Well, I got this commission from Christ."],[2325.88,"I was sent out to all the nations."],[2328.78,"So he says, So I'm talking to you as the person who Christ sent to you."],[2333.95,"Speak to you."],[2336.11,"And he's saying, but my ministry is magnified."],[2339.07,"He didn't send me just to you."],[2341.0,"Where I'm not allowed to talk to my own people."],[2342.68,"He says, my ministry is even greater."],[2344.62,"It's magnified, it's even greater if through jealousy of what's happening with you, I can get some of my own people to come and uh become Christian and join."],[2353.02,"come Some have, or you wouldn't be having the problem of right."],[2360.36,"But remember, he's concerned about there being some kind of separation between the two."],[2364.84,"So he says, even though I'm the apostle to the Gentiles, that doesn't mean I have some kind of separate church or separate, or I only talk to we're all together."],[2374.92,"We're all together."],[2376.52,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2383.88,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2389.24,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.84,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Romans 8 - sons of God, heirs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Romans 11</b>; quick run through the section that begins in <b>ch. 8</b> (8, 9, 10)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 8</b>: theme of becoming <b>sons of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"sons of God\" = a particular <b>rank/type of angels</b> in the OT</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"nice, God's our dad\" - God's family is a <b>royal family</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sons → <b>heirs along with Christ</b> of the kingdom</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ = <b>the heir</b>, <b>firstborn of all things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we inherit <b>and God shares ruling over it with us</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why \"sons\" not \"children\" (he uses both; John more often \"children\")</li><li class=\"d1\">1: title for that class of angels - not men and women</li><li class=\"d1\">2: in Paul's culture <b>the son inherited</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians, men and women, become <b>sons = heirs</b> - no division, <b>co-heirs with Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":195.81,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Romans 9 - Jacob, Esau, Edom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 9</b>: Jacob and Esau - two quotes side by side</li><li class=\"d1\">before birth: <b>older will serve the younger</b> (twins; Esau firstborn, the heir)</li><li class=\"d1\">centuries later in the prophets: \"<b>Jacob I have loved, Esau I have hated</b>\" = nation of <b>Edom</b> vs <b>Israel</b> (Jacob's other name)</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul appeals to the <b>whole history of the nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deuteronomy 2</b>: don't attack Edom, not a foot of their land - <b>brothers</b>; part of land promised to Abraham</li><li class=\"d1\">later Edom a <b>vassal</b> of Israel - older serves younger</li><li class=\"d1\">Edom shared the inheritance <b>while faith and fellowship was kept</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>Assyria</b> destroys the north, <b>Babylon</b> destroys Judah → Edomites <b>attacked their brothers</b>, grabbed land → <b>lost their inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Edom today? <b>Southern Jordan</b> - <b>Petra</b> was an Edomite capital</li></ul>"},{"t":357.85,"lv":3,"h":"The analogy for Israel and Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Question Paul was answering: if Jesus is <b>King of the Jews</b>, why did so many Jews <b>reject</b> him?</li><li class=\"d0\">Like Edomites: descendants of Abraham, but <b>cut themselves off from the heir, Jesus Christ</b> → lose inheritance</li><li class=\"d0\">Inheritance from Abraham depended on <b>keeping faith with God's firstborn Israel</b>; now on <b>keeping faith with Christ, the firstborn</b></li><li class=\"d1\">back to ch. 8: <b>co-heirs by keeping faith with Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nothing received just for being descended from Abraham or not</li></ul>"},{"t":437.24,"lv":3,"h":"The open question: why did God do it?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">End of ch. 9: the question he'll finish in ch. 11 - <b>why did God do what he did with Israel?</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All <b>twelve tribes</b> out of Egypt → couple hundred years later <b>ten tribes gone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why bring them out, give a government, <b>knowing</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jeroboam son of Nebat</b>: \"keep my laws, I'll establish your dynasty like David's\" - God knew he'd be <b>wicked</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Also knew what would happen to <b>Judah and Benjamin</b> - \"Why bother?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So far: God preserved a <b>remnant</b> that embraced Christ - not everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't answer the other <b>~90%</b>: why give blessings to people he knew would fail?</li><li class=\"d1\">only answered so far: \"why did he ever do anything good for them?\" - weird question</li><li class=\"d0\">But Paul <b>does</b> think there's a purpose - ch. 11 finishes the answer</li></ul>"},{"t":603.82,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Romans 10 - Gentiles and faithfulness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 10</b>: many cut off from Israel; <b>Gentiles</b> now come to faith in Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith = faithfulness, loyalty</b>, ongoing way we live - not \"I believe Jesus is Messiah\"</li><li class=\"d1\">cut off = <b>broke faith with Christ</b>, cut themselves off</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles faithful now → part, <b>co-heirs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of ch. 10: prophecies the Gentiles would come in</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deuteronomy</b>: provoke to jealousy by those who are not a nation</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaiah</b>: found by those who did not seek me</li><li class=\"d1\">hands stretched out all day to a disobedient people (attributed here to <b>Joel</b>) - <b>God didn't turn his back; they turned theirs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if willing to come to Christ, <b>part of what he's doing</b></li></ul>"},{"t":731.31,"lv":2,"h":"Israel vs the Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One last note: Paul uses things differently, like <b>body vs flesh</b> - also <b>Israel vs the Jews</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Modern world treats as same, esp. <b>modern political discourse</b>: criticize the country = \"anti-Semitic\"</li><li class=\"d0\">In Paul's day <b>no nation called Israel</b> - not for <b>750 years</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"The Jews\"</b> = <i>Judeans</i>, people of the Roman province of <b>Judea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">usually includes <b>Galileans</b> and that ethnicity elsewhere</li><li class=\"d1\">trivia: <b>Jesus technically not a Jew</b> - Galilean, different province</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Israel\"</b> = speaking <b>theologically</b>: all <b>twelve tribes</b>, as in OT, \"the <b>Israel of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ten of those tribes <b>do not exist</b> in Paul's day</li><li class=\"d0\">Hammering it: Israel in ch. 11 has <b>nothing to do with any nation</b> that has or will exist, <b>nothing to do with ethnicity</b></li></ul>"},{"t":882.75,"lv":3,"h":"Prophecies of all twelve tribes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Prophecies in <b>Isaiah, Amos</b>, others: God restoring his people = <b>all twelve tribes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tribes that no longer existed when written - <b>Ephraim</b> (central, biggest northern tribe, capital) restored</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's problem: how fulfilled when <b>no Manasseh, Ephraim, Dan</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Answer coming in ch. 11</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b>: St. John lists all the tribes - which don't exist</li><li class=\"d0\">All NT writers see them <b>fulfilled</b>; <b>none</b> sees them fulfilled in a <b>Jewish nation state</b> - \"not one of them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fulfilled by replacement? - \"we'll get there\"</li></ul>"},{"t":985.63,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 11:1 - Has God cast away his people?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Has God cast away his people? <b>Certainly not</b>\" - Paul: Israelite, seed of Abraham, tribe of <b>Benjamin</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Me genoito</i> - <b>optative</b>, outdated form; ~60 in NT, <b>40+ by Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"may it never be\" - <b>KJV \"God forbid\"</b> - something we never want to happen</li><li class=\"d0\">Same point as end of ch. 10</li><li class=\"d1\">one interpretation: <b>God is done with Israel</b>, now Gentiles, OT over, starting over</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>not so</b> - he himself is <b>proof</b>, from Benjamin, faithful follower of Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">God has not cast away his people <b>whom he foreknew</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1093.12,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 11:2-4 - Elijah and the 7,000","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Elijah <b>pleads against Israel</b>: killed prophets, torn down altars, \"<b>I alone am left</b>\" → God: \"<b>7,000 who have not bowed the knee to Baal</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Context: right after <b>Mount Carmel</b> showdown with prophets of <b>Baal</b></li><li class=\"d1\">both build altars, <b>fire from heaven</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"hate to ruin Sunday school\" - fire from heaven = how they referred to <b>lightning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">important: <b>no rain for years</b> - Baal a <b>storm god</b>; they'd left their God for him</li><li class=\"d1\">real God: \"okay, no more rain\" - Baal, Zeus etc. depicted holding <b>lightning bolt</b></li><li class=\"d0\">After winning, Elijah slew many prophets of Baal, then went off alone - <b>depressed come-down</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Why don't you kill me?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God rebukes: <b>not the only one</b>, thousands faithful</li></ul>"},{"t":1253.02,"lv":3,"h":"The remnant is nothing new","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Probably the <b>first place</b> in Hebrew scriptures with the idea of a <b>remnant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Majority apostate in Elijah's day - <b>always</b> true; but God always <b>preserved a remnant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: looks like all the Jews turned from <b>Messiah</b> - truth: <b>not all</b></li><li class=\"d1\">about the <b>same proportion</b> as through Israel's history - \"the faithful few are a faithful few\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1312.1,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 11:5-6 - Election of grace","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Remnant <b>according to the election of grace</b>\"; if by grace, <b>not works</b>, else grace no longer grace</li><li class=\"d0\">\"A nice tongue twister\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grace = God working</b>, God's action</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul all along: <b>God's action</b>, not human action</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>predestined</b> = literally <b>put in order before time</b> (sometimes \"foreordained\")</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ died for me before I existed</b>; salvation prepared because he created us and knew</li><li class=\"d0\">Remnant is <b>not just better</b> - preserved because \"I've preserved for myself\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Messiah coming to them = <b>grace</b>; they didn't earn it or make him come</li><li class=\"d0\">Against the <b>Pharisees' math</b></li><li class=\"d1\">exile because of sin → when all keep law perfectly, Messiah comes and saves</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>exact opposite</b> - God takes them out of slavery, gives the land, <b>then</b> gives the law, expects <b>faithfulness</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1484.92,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: \"Very few go to heaven\" sign","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: church sign - \"<b>very few go to heaven</b>\" - sounds like <b>remnant</b> talk?</li><li class=\"d0\">We <b>don't know the numbers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Likely <b>Christ's parable</b>: broad way to destruction, <b>narrow and difficult</b> way to life, few find it</li><li class=\"d1\">core idea: kingdom not reached by <b>easy, lazy life</b>, takes <b>effort</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"come to church or you'll wander off to destruction\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not the same idea: <b>remnant = particular thing in OT prophecy about Israel</b> - wouldn't apply it now</li><li class=\"d1\">some Protestant groups think they're the <b>faithful remnant</b> - \"Faithful Remnant Baptist Church\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's remnant = remnant of <b>OT Israel</b>, purified, <b>basis and root for the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Gentiles coming in? - \"we're getting there\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1662.74,"lv":3,"h":"Why he raises grace and works again","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">To prevent <b>misinterpretation</b>: a Pharisee could say \"a group of us <b>kept the law</b> perfectly, so God sent the Messiah for us and doesn't care about the rest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that <b>God only cares about the elect</b> - a misinterpretation</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>no</b> - not the basis for who accepted Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Already argued: Pharisees' <b>self-righteousness</b> was a big part of why they <b>rejected</b> Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":1734.82,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 11:7-10 - Spirit of stupor","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Israel has not attained what it seeks, <b>the elect</b> have; the rest were <b>blinded</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>spirit of stupor</b>, eyes that should not see\"; <b>David</b>: \"let their table become a snare\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some read: God chose some for salvation, <b>deliberately blinded</b> the rest - no choices</li><li class=\"d1\">contradicts what he just said: <b>hands stretched out all day</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>Romans 1</b>: Gentiles rejected God, worshiped others, so God <b>gave them over</b> to sins</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"don't care about Greeks, Romans, Chinese - send to hell\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they <b>didn't want him</b> → <b>gave them what they wanted</b> = destruction, havoc</li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles knew those things were wrong - <b>result of rejecting the only true God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1912.31,"lv":3,"h":"Blindness as result of their own choices","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same as <b>John's gospel</b>: reaction to Jesus based on <b>who they already were</b></li><li class=\"d1\">seeking God, faithful → <b>recognized him instantly</b>, became disciples</li><li class=\"d1\">wicked in heart → <b>rejected him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Because they'd rejected God, he gave them over → <b>Messiah in front of them</b> and they couldn't see</li><li class=\"d1\">blindness = result of <b>own choices, own faithlessness</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharaoh</b> in Exodus: first <b>hardens his own heart</b>, then \"God hardens his heart\"</li><li class=\"d1\">point comes: \"if this is what you want, you're gonna get all of it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Great threat to pray against: <b>God gives us what we think we want</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"rather than trusting him to give us what we <b>need</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Joke: stop buying lottery tickets? - \"Publisher's Clearinghouse, same chance\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We say \"I really need this\" and get mad; <b>thankful</b> for unanswered prayers - \"would have been a disaster\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Negative side: <b>terrible thing</b> for those separated from Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":2097.1,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 11:11-12 - Their fall, riches for the world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Have they stumbled that they should <b>fall</b>? <b>Certainly not</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">through their fall, <b>jealousy</b>; salvation to the Gentiles</li><li class=\"d1\">hope: seeing Gentiles come to faith → <b>jealousy</b> → draws them back; God <b>fulfilling promises</b> among Gentiles</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Their fall riches for the world... <b>how much more their fullness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if Gentiles coming in is cause to rejoice, how much more when <b>they</b> come to Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Still attacking idea God said \"<b>OT over, starting over</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We don't know how God might bring them back</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Paul himself</b> persecuted the church, was cut off, came to faithfulness → \"if he can, anybody can\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2212.38,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 11:13-14 - Apostle to the Gentiles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Argument so far</li><li class=\"d1\">not done with Israel: there's a <b>remnant</b>, \"me, but not just me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not done with those who haven't accepted: <b>Jewish people have become Christian all through history</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"I magnify my ministry, if by any means I may <b>provoke to jealousy</b> those who are my flesh and save some\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Occasion of the letter: <b>Claudius expelled the Jews from Rome</b> - Gentile Christians stayed <b>over a year</b>, Jews now returning</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul wants <b>one church</b>, not a Jewish and a Gentile church</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Apostle = one sent</b>, close to <b>prophet</b> - sent; most OT prophets sent to Israel; Paul sent to the <b>nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ministry <b>magnified</b> if through jealousy he wins some of his own people</li><li class=\"d1\">not a separate church - \"we're all together\"</li></ul>"}]});
